question: What is the highest premium policy in [specific line of business]?
cot: CoT: Q: What is the highest premium policy in [specific line of business]? A: The primary data is provided through the custom PostgreSQL function 'get_premiums()'. This custom function internally calculates the premium of the policy and returns: policy_id : This is the policy identifier policy_reference: This is the reference of the policy is_endorsement : Boolean field which indicates if the policy is an endorsement or not status_group : which has 2 options 'written' and 'not_written' premium: the amount of the policy premium We started the query with a CTE to use a Window function 'ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY elob.name ORDER BY gp.premium DESC nulls last) AS row_num' where the field called 'elob.name' is the line_of_business. This CTE sort the premium from highest to lowest of each status and be sure to pull the null values to the end since to return a numeric value is mandatory. Finally, we select the main fields to return 'policy_reference, status_group, line_of_business, premium, row_num' from our CTE and select the first already-ordered result. The SQL query is: WITH RANKED_PREMIUMS AS (SELECT gp.policy_reference, gp.status_group, elob.name AS line_of_business, gp.premium, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY elob.name ORDER BY gp.premium DESC NULLS LAST) AS row_num FROM get_premiums() gp LEFT JOIN policies_policymodel pm ON gp.policy_id = pm.id LEFT JOIN policies_policycoveragemodel pcm ON pm.id = pcm.policy_id LEFT JOIN entities_lineofbusinessmodel elob ON pcm.line_of_business_id = elob.id) SELECT policy_reference, status_group, line_of_business, premium, row_num FROM ranked_premiums WHERE row_num = 1 ORDER BY line_of_business ASC; 
sql_query: WITH RANKED_PREMIUMS AS (SELECT gp.policy_reference, gp.status_group, elob.name AS line_of_business, gp.premium, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY elob.name ORDER BY gp.premium DESC NULLS LAST) AS row_num FROM get_premiums() gp LEFT JOIN policies_policymodel pm ON gp.policy_id = pm.id LEFT JOIN policies_policycoveragemodel pcm ON pm.id = pcm.policy_id LEFT JOIN entities_lineofbusinessmodel elob ON pcm.line_of_business_id = elob.id) SELECT policy_reference, status_group, line_of_business, premium, row_num FROM ranked_premiums WHERE row_num = 1 ORDER BY line_of_business ASC;

question: Can you list the top 10 policies by premium amount?
cot: CoT: Q: Can you list the top 10 policies by premium amount? A: The primary data is provided through the custom PostgreSQL function 'get_premiums()'. This custom function internally calculates the premium of the policy and returns: policy_id : This is the policy identifier policy_reference: This is the reference of the policy is_endorsement : Boolean field which indicates if the policy is an endorsement or not status_group : which has 2 options 'written' and 'not_written' premium: the amount of the policy premium We started the query with a CTE to use a Window function 'ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY status_group ORDER BY premium DESC nulls last) AS row_num'. This CTE sort the premium from highest to lowest of each status and be sure to pull the null values to the end since to return a numeric value is mandatory. Finally, we select the main fields to return 'policy_reference, status_group, premiu, row_num' from our CTE and select the first already-ordered result. The SQL query is: WITH RANKED_PREMIUMS AS (SELECT policy_reference, status_group, premium, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY status_group ORDER BY premium DESC NULLS LAST) AS row_num FROM get_premiums()) SELECT policy_reference, status_group, premium, row_num FROM ranked_premiums WHERE row_num BETWEEN 1 AND 10 ORDER BY premium DESC;
sql_query: WITH RANKED_PREMIUMS AS (SELECT policy_reference, status_group, premium, ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY status_group ORDER BY premium DESC NULLS LAST) AS row_num FROM get_premiums()) SELECT policy_reference, status_group, premium, row_num FROM ranked_premiums WHERE row_num BETWEEN 1 AND 10 ORDER BY premium DESC;

question: How many policies are below 2000$ premium?
cot: CoT: Q: How many policies are below 2000$ premium? A: The primary data is provided through the custom PostgreSQL function 'get_premiums()'. This custom function internally calculates the premium of the policy and returns: policy_id : This is the policy identifier policy_reference: This is the reference of the policy is_endorsement : Boolean field which indicates if the policy is an endorsement or not status_group : which has 2 options 'written' and 'not_written' premium: the amount of the policy premium The query with a CTE _threshold that sets the premium threshold to be compared with. Then, select all the premiums from get_premiums() function to get all policies with a join to the _threshold CTE for making calculations. We perform a count on the policy_id, select the threshold to show it in the final answer and make two calculus: policies_below_threshold: integer that holds the number of policies with premium below the threshold Finally select the status_group field. The SQL query is: WITH _threshold AS (SELECT 2000 AS threshold) SELECT COUNT(cp.policy_reference) AS total_policies, th.threshold AS threshold, COUNT(CASE WHEN cp.premium < th.threshold THEN 1 END) AS policies_below_threshold, status_group FROM get_premiums() cp JOIN _threshold th ON TRUE GROUP BY th.threshold, status_group, th.threshold;
sql_query: WITH _threshold AS (SELECT 2000 AS threshold) SELECT COUNT(cp.policy_reference) AS total_policies, th.threshold AS threshold, COUNT(CASE WHEN cp.premium < th.threshold THEN 1 END) AS policies_below_threshold, status_group FROM get_premiums() cp JOIN _threshold th ON TRUE GROUP BY th.threshold, status_group, th.threshold;

question: How many policies are below 2000$ premium?
cot: CoT: Q: What percentage of policies are below 2000$ premium? A: The primary data is provided through the custom PostgreSQL function 'get_premiums()'. This custom function internally calculates the premium of the policy and returns: policy_id : This is the policy identifier policy_reference: This is the reference of the policy is_endorsement : Boolean field which indicates if the policy is an endorsement or not status_group : which has 2 options 'written' and 'not_written' premium: the amount of the policy premium The query with a CTE _threshold that sets the premium threshold to be compared with. Then, select all the premiums from get_premiums() function to get all policies with a join to the _threshold CTE for making calculations. We perform a count on the policy_id, select the threshold to show it in the final answer and make two calculus: policies_below_threshold: integer that holds the number of policies with premium below the threshold policies_below_threshold_percentage: numeric value that holds the percentage of the policies_below_threshold vs the total_policies. Finally select the status_group field. The SQL query is: WITH _threshold AS (SELECT 2000 AS threshold) SELECT COUNT(cp.policy_id) AS total_policies, th.threshold AS threshold, COUNT(CASE WHEN cp.premium < th.threshold THEN 1 END) AS policies_below_threshold, ROUND((COUNT(CASE WHEN cp.premium < th.threshold THEN 1 END) * 100.0 / COUNT(cp.policy_reference)), 2) AS policies_below_threshold_percentage, status_group FROM get_premiums() cp JOIN _threshold th ON TRUE WHERE cp.policy_id IS NOT NULL GROUP BY th.threshold, status_group;
sql_query: WITH _threshold AS (SELECT 2000 AS threshold) SELECT COUNT(cp.policy_id) AS total_policies, th.threshold AS threshold, COUNT(CASE WHEN cp.premium < th.threshold THEN 1 END) AS policies_below_threshold, ROUND((COUNT(CASE WHEN cp.premium < th.threshold THEN 1 END) * 100.0 / COUNT(cp.policy_reference)), 2) AS policies_below_threshold_percentage, status_group FROM get_premiums() cp JOIN _threshold th ON TRUE WHERE cp.policy_id IS NOT NULL GROUP BY th.threshold, status_group;"]

